# Load generator options.
#
# SIM_BLOCK is the one setting that MUST be unique per board. Two boards
# sharing a block emit the same addresses, the server dedupes them as one
# device, and the fleet silently appears half the size it should be — which
# looks exactly like 50% packet loss. Set it from a fragment (tag01.conf ..),
# never by editing a file between flashes.

menu "Aetherfield load generator"

config SIM_DEVICES
	int "Virtual devices emitted by this board"
	default 150
	range 1 500
	help
	  Each gets its own address, its own slot in the beacon cycle and its
	  own last-cmd-seq echo. 20 boards x 150 = 3000.

config SIM_BLOCK
	int "Address block index for this board (-1 = derive at runtime)"
	default -1
	range -1 408
	help
	  Addresses are AURA_ADDR_BAND_BASE + block*SIM_DEVICES + i.

	  Leave this at -1 and the board derives its block from the factory
	  device id at boot, which means ONE binary flashes to all twenty
	  boards. That is worth a great deal: the old scheme needed twenty
	  Kconfig fragments and twenty full Zephyr builds to vary one integer.

	  The cost is that derivation is a birthday problem. Twenty boards into
	  409 blocks clash with probability 37.6%, and a clash is invisible in
	  the worst way — two boards emit the same addresses, the server dedupes
	  them into one device, and the fleet looks 150 devices short, which
	  reads as packet loss rather than as a numbering fault.

	  So the board prints its block at boot, the server reports how many
	  distinct blocks it can see, and 'sim block <n>' moves a board over RTT
	  without rebuilding. Set this to a fixed value only if you want one
	  particular board pinned.

config SIM_BEACON_MS
	int "Unused — kept so existing fragments still parse"
	default 10000
	range 100 60000
	help
	  The beacon interval now comes from the commanded op_param over the
	  air (op_param x 100 ms, 0 meaning AURA_BEACON_DEFAULT_MS), exactly as
	  it does on a real Pixel. Set it for the whole fleet at once from the
	  dashboard with 'aura op 0 <param>'; override one board locally with
	  'sim rate <ms>'.

	  Twenty boards on hardware with no UART is twenty RTT sessions, and a
	  sweep where each board changes rate at a different moment is a smear
	  rather than a measurement.

config SIM_CSMA
	bool "Use CSMA/CA instead of direct transmit"
	default n
	help
	  The shipping firmware transmits with IEEE802154_TX_MODE_DIRECT — it
	  does not listen before it talks, which makes the uplink unslotted
	  ALOHA. ALOHA peaks around 18% channel utilisation and gets WORSE as
	  offered load rises beyond that.

	  Switch live with 'sim csma 1' and compare delivery rates at the same
	  offered load. That comparison is the most useful number this whole
	  test produces.

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
